I resolved the timing data-dependency findings raised by the security gate. The `canon_nan` function in the C model previously contained a conditional branch (`if (exp == 0xFF && frac != 0)`) that depended on operand values, which created a timing and side-channel leak. I replaced this with branchless, masked selection logic (`uint32_t is_nan = (exp == 0xFF) & (frac != 0); uint32_t m = (uint32_t)0 - is_nan; return (0x7FC00000 & m) | (bits & ~m);`). This guarantees that `fmac_custom` takes a strictly constant number of cycles across all inputs, addressing both the `timing.data_dependent` and `timing.secret_branch` blockers while retaining the instruction's performance and design.